Chapter: VmxInitTest
  cites Works chapter DevHypervisor

 The VMX gate answers what this machine actually is.

 This test used to assert `vmx-enable:ok` while `vmx-enable` returned
 `VmxOk` unconditionally -- a test that could not fail, on a function that
 could not answer anything else, behind a `.skip` claiming it needed real
 hardware. Both halves of that are real now: the gate reads
 IA32_FEATURE_CONTROL, and this runs in the battery.

 THE EXPECTED OUTPUT IS THIS MACHINE'S, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The battery
 runs under codex-vm, whose firmware reports VT-x locked off, so the right
 answer here is `fail`. A run on a workstation with VT-x enabled in the
 BIOS would print `ok`, and that is not a broken test -- it is the gate
 doing its job on a different machine. What would be broken is `ok` HERE,
 because that is the old defect exactly: a machine that cannot enter VMX
 operation being told that it did.

 The reason line is printed because a refusal that does not say what to do
 about it sends somebody to read source. It names the MSR and its value,
 so the same output separates "firmware turned VT-x off" from "firmware
 never configured it" without a debugger.

 We say:

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let rev = vmx-read-revision-id
   in let status = vmx-enable rev
   in let vmcs-status = vmx-create-vmcs rev
   in act
    print-line-uni ("vmx-available:" & show vmx-available)
    print-line-uni (when status is VmxOk -> "vmx-enable:ok" is VmxFailed (c) -> "vmx-enable:fail")
    print-line-uni (when vmcs-status is VmxOk -> "vmcs-create:ok" is VmxFailed (c) -> "vmcs-create:fail")
    print-line-uni ("reason: " & vmx-unavailable-reason)
    print-line-uni "vmx-init:done"
   end
  end
